      "writtenOn": "2026-10-01T12:26:55Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "This dma-buf could be immediately freed on another thread when you release the lock, right? Should you increment the refcount before passing the pointer to the caller?",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 786,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 790,
        "endChar": 9
      },
      "revId": "e0870895e8e1a26e46802f4e2f18817f119918eb",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "7fcff592_b422f6f9",
        "filename": "drivers/android/wrapfd/wrap.rs",
        "patchSetId": 28
      },
      "lineNbr": 790,
      "author": {
        "id": 1014088
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-10-01T13:27:58Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "The original code doesn\u0027t. And I guess anything that would free the dma-buf would be a \"modification\" and thus guarded by \"check_modifiable\" which would return false, since we are owned by a device when the guard is released. That said I wonder if we should acquire a referecne on the dmabuf here and release it in \"put_mappable\". @isaacmanjarres@google.com, @surenb@google.com",
      "parentUuid": "438c5f13_0471c750",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 786,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 790,
        "endChar": 9
      },
      "revId": "e0870895e8e1a26e46802f4e2f18817f119918eb",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "0bfc74e1_4d3f154b",
        "filename": "drivers/android/wrapfd/wrap.rs",
        "patchSetId": 28
      },
      "lineNbr": 790,
      "author": {
        "id": 1157738
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-10-01T16:05:19Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "To call `wrapfd_get_mappable` we should have a stable `wrapfd` file which should remain valid until we call `wrapfd_put_mappable`. wrapfd already holds a refcount for that dmabuf, so it should not go away from under us.\n\nThinking a bit more...\nI guess the case you are considering is when userspace closes the wrapfd file without calling `wrapfd_put_mappable` (which is technically a userspace bug but we don\u0027t want to crash the whole system if this happens)? I guess taking a dmabuf refcount here would result in a memory leak vs a possible UAF it we don\u0027t. That\u0027s definitely a better alternative, so taking a refcount on dmabuf in get_mappable and dropping it in put_mappable seems reasonable to me.",
      "parentUuid": "7fcff592_b422f6f9",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 786,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 790,
        "endChar": 9
      },
      "revId": "e0870895e8e1a26e46802f4e2f18817f119918eb",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "1c9820ef_9ca14de5",
        "filename": "drivers/android/wrapfd/wrap.rs",
        "patchSetId": 28
      },
      "lineNbr": 790,
      "author": {
        "id": 1934715
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-10-01T16:48:42Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e I guess the case you are considering is when userspace closes the wrapfd file without calling wrapfd_put_mappable (which is technically a userspace bug but we don\u0027t want to crash the whole system if this happens)? \n\nWouldn\u0027t the driver still have a reference? I was imagining the flow in the scenario you\u0027re describing to be something like:\n\n- Userspace acquires wrapfd (lets assume the dmabuf fd is closed after wrapfd is acquired):  wrapfd refcount: 1 dmabuf refcount: 1\n- Kernel driver acquires wrapfd from userspace via an ioctl and gets the file structure associated with the wrapfd: wrapfd refcount: 2 dmabuf refcount: 1\n- Kernel driver invokes `wrapfd_get_mappable()`: no change in refcounts\n- Userspace closes wrapfd: wrapfd refcount: 1 dma buf refcount: 1\n\nThere\u0027s a problem if the driver calls `fput()` on the wrapfd file structure before calling `wrapfd_get_mappable()`, but that\u0027s a driver bug, and the driver should fix that?",
      "parentUuid": "0bfc74e1_4d3f154b",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 786,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 790,
        "endChar": 9
      },
      "revId": "e0870895e8e1a26e46802f4e2f18817f119918eb",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
